## v0.25.11

### MSG-2 Phase 1 follow-up — authority hardening

- `moderation_state` no longer raises when the authority fixpoint hits
  AUTHORITY_PASSES_CAP: the topic flags `authority_unresolved` on
  `/rpc/messaging/topics` and `/rpc/messaging/topics/{slug}/moderation`
  and answers best-effort — a long grant chain cannot 500 topic reads.
- Property test pins the fixpoint to a brute-force canonical-order replay
  over all grant/revoke rows (40 randomised grant/revoke/reorder seeds);
  a cap-tripped topic test proves reads still answer.


### MSG-8d — Phase 1: encrypted direct messages implemented

- Predicate (`aitbc/messaging.py`): new kinds `dm` (`to` + `ct` fields)
  and `dm_key` (`enc_pubkey`, strict base64 of a 32-byte X25519 key);
  reserved slug `dm` enforced bidirectionally for both DM kinds —
  `topic == "dm"` iff `kind in DM_KINDS` (dm_key carries the slug too,
  so the carve-outs stay uniform; the design text names the rule for
  `dm` only — this is the one reading that keeps dm_key out of forum
  aggregates). `ct` capped at 4096 base64 chars; strict decode.
- Index (`messaging_index.py`): single `is_dm_row` helper; candidate
  SQL excludes the `dm` slug from every non-DM branch so legacy squats
KEEP
  paging means invalid rows can never hide a valid key.
- RPC: `GET /rpc/messaging/dm-keys/{address}` (404 when none) and
  `GET /rpc/messaging/dm/{address}` (cursor-paged inbox).
- CLI: `aitbc messaging dm keygen|send|inbox|read`; X25519 keys live in
  mode-600 `<wallets>/../dm-keys/<wallet>.json` (eras accumulate for
  rotation); send is dry-run by default and refuses when the recipient
  has no published key; plaintext cap ~2920 B enforced before signing;
  read verifies the `aitbc-dm-v1` inner header (context + sender +
  recipient) — rejects on any mismatch, never prints unverified text.
- No forward secrecy (accepted Phase-1 limit; v2 work is parked).
- Tests: 42 new — predicate (kinds, caps, bidirectional slug, bad
  recipients/keys), index carve-outs + legacy-row hiding, inbox
  ordering/cursor/dm_key rotation + same-block tie, CLI crypto
  (roundtrip, tamper, wrong key, header/context/UTF-8/empty rejects)
  and the key-file store.
- Pre-rollout chain check: 15 sealed MESSAGE txs on live main; zero
  carry a `dm` topic or DM field — the reserved-slug rule lands on a
  clean slate. Deployment itself stays separately gated.


### MSG-8e — DM key-store hardening (before any dm_key is published)

- Persist-before-publish, once: `aitbc.dm_client.dm_prepare_era` /
  `dm_mark_published` are the shared helpers both callers (CLI `dm
  keygen`, SDK `dm_publish_key`) use — a fresh era is written with
  `published_tx: null` before the envelope is submitted, so a crash can
  never strand a published public half. A failed submit keeps the era
  on disk unpublished and the next run re-publishes the same era
  (verified by crash-simulation tests on both paths).
- Atomic secret write: `dm-keys/` created 0700; the store is written to
  `<name>.tmp` via `os.open(O_CREAT|O_EXCL|O_WRONLY, 0o600)` then
  `os.replace`d — no wider-mode window under any umask, no backup copies.
- Corrupt store refused: `dm_store_load(strict=True)` (all save paths,
  `_dm_current_key`, SDK read-key resolution) raises
  `DmStoreCorruptError` on parse/read errors or a non-list `keys` and
  leaves the file byte-identical — never overwritten.
- `dm keygen` without `--rotate` on an already-published key now returns
  after the notice instead of signing a redundant `dm_key` envelope
  (finding 4).
- Design doc `msg8-direct-messages.md` gained the key-file safety rules.
  Tests: 15 new across CLI + SDK (crash simulations, mode assertions
  under umask 022/000, byte-identical corrupt files, retry reuse).


### WAL-3: the last three non-standard secret paths join WAL-1 (2026-10-15)

Three leftovers closed out the shared wallet-secret standard.

`wallet bridge sell quote` and `sell lock` load the node wallet through
`_resolve_wallet` but could not take `--password-file`/`--password-stdin`,
leaving headless agents with only env vars and the keyring. Both commands
now carry `@wallet_password_options` and forward the trio into
`_load_wallet`'s lazy resolver — the secret resolves exactly once and an
unencrypted wallet still never asks. `sell status` never touches a wallet
file and is unchanged.

`wallet/staking.py`'s liquidity re-save paths still fell back to the
pre-WAL-1 `_get_wallet_password` (keyring, env, a private two-prompt TTY
loop) whenever the resolved password was falsy — dead code in practice,
since an encrypted wallet already failed closed in the resolver. The
fallback is gone and `_get_wallet_password` itself is deleted, removing
the last private `getpass` in `commands/`.

`genesis init --password` appended the secret to the
`unified_genesis.py` subprocess argv (visible in `ps` and shell history)
and `genesis reset` took `--password` as a bare option. Both commands now
expose the standard trio — `--password` deprecated with the usual
warning, plus `--password-file`/`--password-stdin` — and resolve through
`resolve_new_wallet_password` with the TTY prompt off. `init` hands the
value to the child by `AITBC_GENESIS_PASSWORD` in its environment
(child-only, zero filesystem lifetime — the script already writes
`.genesis_password` 0600, so env adds nothing weaker); the script reads
the env var when no `--password` argv is given, keeping direct
invocation working. `init` refuses a `--password*` flag without
`--create-wallet` — an ignored credential flag is the wrong failure
mode. `reset` keeps auto-generating when no flag is given.

Tests: `cli/tests/test_wal3_secret_leftovers.py` — 18 cases over real
encrypted wallets (`_save_wallet`, real resolvers): file/stdin flag
signing and broadcast for `sell lock`, wrong-password and no-password
fail-closed, stdin read exactly once, secret absence from output and
logs, `sell quote` unlock/fail-closed, the staking re-save round-trip
plus headless fail-closed before submit, genesis init's env-not-argv
boundary on all three sources, reset's resolved/None hand-off, and the
script-side `AITBC_GENESIS_PASSWORD` consumption with argv precedence.

Commits: `2d265c090` (sell trio + staking resolver + genesis env
hand-off + MCP regen + tests).


### fix(website): WEB-9 — client-side rendering safety audit + hardening (2026-10-15)

- Audit: enumerated every DOM sink across website/*.js and inline
  scripts (60 innerHTML writes, insertAdjacentHTML in blocks.js,
  dynamic href/src setters, storage use). Every data-bearing
  interpolation resolves to escapeHtml/encodeURIComponent/
  textContent or a numeric/class constant — no unescaped
  attacker-controlled sink found; jsdom proofs with hostile stub
  payloads stayed inert (scratch/web9/, never committed).
- Hardening: every target="_blank" link (13 static + generated)
  now carries rel="noopener noreferrer" (8 files).
- Guard: tests/unit/test_website_client_sinks.py — offline static
  scan; every markup-template interpolation must provably resolve
  to an escaping call, constant, or audited allowlist entry
  (stale entries fail); forbids eval/new Function/document.write/
  outerHTML; no external-origin includes; _blank requires
  noopener noreferrer. Red-proof verified by injecting an
  unescaped field. 5/5 green.
- Docs: docs/website-csp-proposal.md — report-only CSP draft
  (default-src 'self', connect-src 'self'), inventories the
  inline scripts/handlers/styles blocking a strict policy, with
  an examples/nginx snippet for review. No nginx change here.


## v0.25.9

### Key-split ordering — all authority resolves to one key (2026-09-29)

The parameter-history audit confirms every authority lands on hub's
``0x02B8F2C6…`` key: ``governance_executors`` (9819),
``escrow_settlement_authority`` (22193), ``bridge_release_authority``
(22469) — only ``bond_slash_authority`` differs (``0xab0797…``).

Split order, ranked by the damage each key can do:

1. **governance_executors** — can reassign every other authority,
   including itself. New dedicated offline key; switched via
   ``parameter_change`` executed by the current executor (last use of
   0x02B8… for governance).
2. **bridge_release_authority** — signs credits that mint funds. New key;
   same change carries the bridge validator-set parameter (4 consensus
   validators, 3-of-4) and the ETH contract validator list+threshold, so
   the contract never points at keys being rotated.
3. **escrow_settlement_authority** — settles locks/refunds.
4. **bond_slash_authority** — already on a separate key; verify it is not
   on a running host.
Plus the non-parameter items: faucet gets its own funded wallet (~1 week
budget); treasury/genesis funds move to an offline address; the proposer
key stays confined to aitbc-blockchain-node only.


