### v9 shadow firing root cause and fix: BRIDGE_LOCK on the parallel path (2026-09-28)

The canary BRIDGE_LOCK (0x6360b047…, height 27251) fired
v9_would_reject_bridge_lock_unsigned_total=1 on all four followers — the
first real v9 bug the shadow window caught: both callers of
compute_state_delta resolved bridge_authority only when the block
contained BRIDGE_RELEASE/BRIDGE_REFUND (sync_block_import.py and
consensus/poa.py), so a block whose only bridge tx is a BRIDGE_LOCK
reached the pure path with bridge_authority=None and
verify_bridge_lock_signature(…, "") returned False — a shadow count at
v8; at v9 activation every follower would have REJECTED a block the
proposer's sequential path (which resolves the authority itself)
accepted — a guaranteed fork. The shadow counter did its job.

Fix:
- state/bridge_credit.py: new BRIDGE_AUTHORITY_TX_TYPES =
  BRIDGE_CREDIT_TX_TYPES | {"BRIDGE_LOCK"} — the single set callers use
  to decide whether to resolve the bridge release authority for a block,
  so the two predicates cannot drift again.
- sync_block_import.py and consensus/poa.py parallel-apply callers now
  resolve the authority for any of those types at block_version >= 5.
- poa.py proposal admission: pre-registered BRIDGE_LOCK rows previously
  bypassed apply without any signature check — stamping an unsigned or
  invalid lock would have sealed a block followers reject. Now: at v9 the
  row is skipped with a warning (failed v9 bridge-lock authorization);
  below v9 an unsigned/invalid lock counts
  v9_would_reject_bridge_lock_unsigned once and is still stamped.
- tests/test_v9_signature_rules.py: four parallel-path cases — v9 signed
  lock applies with no v9 counter; v9 unsigned lock is not applied; v8
  signed lock applies with NO shadow counter (the exact regression); v8
  unsigned lock applies and counts once.

The v9 shadow window restarts again on deploy: the
v9_would_reject_bridge_lock_unsigned counter must read absent after the
rolling restart (counters reset on service restart).

### Fleet state-digest monitor, aux case-fix, governance-vote dedupe, and pure/sequential parity (2026-09-29)

Semantic state digests across the fleet:
- state/block_deltas.py classifies every chain table: CONSENSUS_STATE_TABLES
  (account, block, transaction, receipt, chain_parameter(+history),
  governance_proposal, bond, gpu_*, ipfs_subscription, liquidity_*),
  AUX_SHIPPED_TABLES (governance_vote, stake — must converge), service-local
  (escrow, bridge_*, mempool, block_state_delta, ...), and the watch member
  cross_chain_transfer (service-local rows that feed block.bridge_state_root
  — drift warns, never fails).
