### V23-85 — the security gate that exited on a usage error, in a hook nothing installed · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Two independent failures, either of which alone would have been enough. The filed finding was
the second one.

#### It could not have passed

```yaml
- id: bandit
  args: [-c, pyproject.toml, -r, ., -x, cli/templates, tests/cli, tests/]
```

`-x` takes one comma-separated value. Written as three separate list items, `cli/templates`
goes to `-x` and `tests/cli` and `tests/` fall through to the positional slot argparse has
already filled with `.`:

```
$ pre-commit run bandit --hook-stage pre-push --all-files
bandit...................................................................Failed
- hook id: bandit
- exit code: 2

bandit: error: unrecognized arguments: tests/cli tests/
```

Exit 2 on a usage error, before opening a file. There is no input on which this hook passes.

#### Nothing installed it either

```
$ ls .git/hooks | grep -v sample
commit-msg  post-checkout  post-commit  post-merge  pre-commit  pre-push

$ cat .git/hooks/pre-push
#!/bin/sh
command -v git-lfs >/dev/null 2>&1 || { ... }
git lfs pre-push "$@"
```

That is git-lfs's hook, not pre-commit's — `.git/hooks/pre-commit` carries the
`# File generated by pre-commit` header and this one does not. `pre-commit install` with no
arguments installs the pre-commit hook and nothing else, and bandit was the only
`stages: [pre-push]` entry in the file. Every document in the repository that tells you how to
set this up says the bare command:

| document | line |
|---|---|
| `docs/development/5_developer-guide.md` | 38 |
| `docs/meta/installation-guide.md` | 67 |
| `docs/agents/AGENTS.md` | 238 |
| `skills/aitbc-software-setup/SKILL.md` | 281 |

Only the archived `docs/releases/v0.4/v0.4.22_PRE_COMMIT_HOOKS_GUIDE.md` mentions
`--hook-type pre-push`, and nothing current points at it.

#### And there was no configuration to read

`-c pyproject.toml` has been in the args since the hook was added. There has never been a
`[tool.bandit]` section in that file — the only occurrence of the string is `bandit = "1.9.4"`
in the dev dependencies. The hook was also pinned at `rev: 1.8.3`, so if it had ever run it
would have run a different bandit from the one the project installs.

#### What it finds

Once invocable:

| scope | findings |
|---|---|
| everything on disk | 4,244 |
| tracked files only | 3,837 |
| tracked, excluding test files | 449 |
| tracked, excluding tests, MEDIUM severity and above | 36 |

The first two rows differ because bandit walks the filesystem and does not read `.gitignore`.
That run already passed `-x` for `venv/`; the 407 findings it still picked up outside version
control were 390 from `apps/coordinator-api/.venv/`, 16 from the vendored forge-std and
OpenZeppelin trees under `contracts/governance/lib/`, and 1 stray script. The `.venv/` share
includes 11 of the 21 HIGH findings, every one of them inside pip — its own `_internal` and
its vendored copies of requests and distlib.

Of the 3,837 tracked findings, 3,388 were `B101 assert_used` in test files. A gate that
reports four thousand things blocks nothing, so the hook now runs at
`--severity-level medium`, and `[tool.bandit]` in `pyproject.toml` holds the exclusions the
broken `-x` was reaching for.

`B104` (binding `0.0.0.0`) is skipped repo-wide with the reason written next to it: eight
sites, every one a service that runs in a container or behind the nginx proxy and binds the
one interface it has. B104 fires on the only spelling that works.

#### The 36

Down to **0**. Fixed rather than silenced, except where the finding was about a shape bandit
cannot see through:

**Real, fixed:**

- `scripts/utils/generate-api-keys.py` masks every key it prints — `print(f"API Key: {'*' * 32}")` —
  and then wrote all of them in cleartext to `/tmp/aitbc-api-keys.json` at the process umask.
  A fixed path any account on the host could read, and could have pre-created as a symlink.
  Now `tempfile.mkdtemp()` plus `os.open(..., O_EXCL, 0o600)`.
- `scripts/extract_openapi_specs.py` and `scripts/generate_openapi.py` set
  `WALLET_DIR=/tmp/test_wallet` — a keystore directory on a guessable shared path, with
  `KEYSTORE_PASSWORD=test-password` beside it. `generate_openapi.py` already had a
  `tempfile.mkdtemp()` two lines above and was not using it for this.
- Five `requests` calls with no timeout in `scripts/utils/cleanup_fake_gpus.py` and
  `scripts/utils/end_to_end_workflow.py`. requests waits forever by default.
- Two `urlopen` calls on URLs built from `$WALLET_RPC_URL` and `$GITEA_API_BASE`, both
  carrying credentials in an Authorization header, neither checking the scheme. `urlopen`
  honours `file:`. Both now reject anything that is not http or https — and both still carry
  `# nosec B310`, because B310 is a blacklist rule on the call and fires whatever guards
  precede it.
- Three staging files in the container deploy scripts written to fixed `/tmp` paths on the way
  to becoming root-owned unit files inside the container.

**False positive, marked:**

- `agent_stream.py:374` B608. The f-string interpolates `','.join('?' * len(spellings))` — a
  run of placeholders, never a value; every address arrives through the parameter tuple.
  sqlite3 has no way to bind a variable-length `IN` list, so this is the supported idiom.
  Lifted into a named `placeholders` variable so the marker sits on something readable.

**Deliberate, marked with the reason:**

- Ten `shell=True` calls across `scripts/deployment/deploy/container-deploy.py`,
  `deploy_container_with_miner.py` and `deploy_gpu_to_container.py`. The commands are literals
  composed with a container name that is the constant `"aitbc"` assigned inline; there is no
  input to inject, and they need a shell for `cd X && source Y && pip install` and
  `2>/dev/null || true`. Each marker carries the condition that invalidates it: a container
  name arriving from a flag or the environment.

#### Two things found on the way, filed separately rather than fixed here

- **22 tracked scripts hardcode `/home/oib/windsurf/aitbc`**, a path from a developer's old
  workstation that does not exist on this host. The three deploy scripts above are among them,
  which is why their `shell=True` calls were marked rather than restructured — they are dead
  code, and deciding that is a different change from making the hook work.
- **`scripts/testing/qa-cycle.py` has never done anything.** `run_cmd` passes command strings
  to `subprocess.run` with `shell=False`, so `"git fetch origin main"` is looked up as a single
  executable name; the resulting `FileNotFoundError` is swallowed by a bare `except`, and
  `main()` aborts at its first step.

#### Fix

`.pre-commit-config.yaml`:

```yaml
default_install_hook_types: [pre-commit, pre-push]
```

```yaml
  - repo: https://github.com/PyCQA/bandit
    rev: 1.9.4
    hooks:
      - id: bandit
        args: [-c, pyproject.toml, -r, ., --severity-level, medium]
```

`rev` moves 1.8.3 → 1.9.4 to match the project's own pin. `commit-msg` is deliberately absent
from `default_install_hook_types`: no hook declares that stage, and installing it would
displace the hand-written PILOT-79 ticket-tag guard for nothing.

`pyproject.toml` gains `[tool.bandit]` with `exclude_dirs` and the single `skips` entry.
Note that bandit matches `exclude_dirs` entries as plain substrings as well as globs, which is
why each is written with its surrounding slashes.

#### Verification

```
$ pre-commit run bandit --hook-stage pre-push --all-files
bandit...................................................................Passed
```

0 HIGH, 0 MEDIUM — down from 36 MEDIUM-and-above. 427 LOW remain below the gate.

`pre-commit run --all-files` passes every other hook, including `mypy-clean-apps`, `ruff`,
`ruff-format` and `openapi-specs-match-the-apps` — the last confirming that swapping
`scripts/extract_openapi_specs.py` onto a temporary `WALLET_DIR` still regenerates the specs
byte-identically. The one failure is `require-set-euo-pipefail-in-touched-scripts`, on 13
pre-existing `.sh` files this change does not touch; that hook is scoped to scripts in the
commit by design (V23-23), so `--all-files` is the wrong way to run it and its failure there
is expected.

Root suite: 6,284 passed, 272 skipped, exit 0 — the full 6,556, unchanged. The only app file
in this change is `agent_stream.py`, and the edit there is a comment plus a `# nosec` marker.

#### Operator step

This clone still has git-lfs's `pre-push` hook, and installing over it is an operator
decision, not one to take inside a shared working tree that deployed services run from:

```bash
pre-commit install
```

With `default_install_hook_types` set, that one command now installs both hook types. It is
safe to run over git-lfs's hook — pre-commit moves an existing hook to `pre-push.legacy` and
runs it before its own, so `git lfs pre-push` keeps happening. Confirmed in
`pre_commit/commands/hook_impl.py`, which calls `_run_legacy` before dispatching.


### V23-87 — a security fix that flipped one keyword, and the script that stopped at its first command · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`scripts/testing/qa-cycle.py` fetches `main`, runs the two package test suites, lints, requests
sibling reviews on open PRs and reports repository status. Every caller of its `run_cmd` passed
a single string:

```python
rc, out, err = run_cmd("git fetch origin main")
```

and `run_cmd` ran it with `shell=False`, which makes `subprocess` treat that string as the name
of one executable. There is no program called `git fetch origin main`, so the first command
raised `FileNotFoundError`, the bare `except Exception` below it returned `(-2, "", str(e))`,
`fetch_latest_main()` logged `Fetch failed` and `main()` returned. The script has never run past
its first command — no tests, no lint, no review requests, no status.

#### Where the string form came from

It was not always wrong. `dcfa58039` (2026-06-06) added the script with `shell=True`, and under
a shell a string is exactly right. `21e142b70` (2026-06-10, *"update mypy config, remove
hardcoded credentials, improve security and logging"*) changed one line:

```
-            shell=True,
+            shell=False,
```

and nothing else. That is the correct fix for B602 and half of it: turning the shell off means
the callers have to become argv lists in the same commit, and they did not. Four days of
working, then sixty-seven of a script that logged one failure and exited.

Nothing caught it because nothing looked. `rc=-2` from the bare `except` is indistinguishable
from a command that ran and failed, and the only reader was the log.

#### Nothing depends on the broken behaviour

Checked before changing it, since a scheduled job that quietly does nothing is still a job
someone may be reading the output of:

- no unit in `/etc/systemd/system` mentions it — 26 services, 2 timers, and the only timers are
  `aitbc-backup` and `aitbc-blockchain-sync`
- `/var/spool/cron/crontabs/` is empty, `crontab -l` reports no crontab for root, and nothing
  under `/etc/cron.d`, `/etc/crontab` or `/etc/cron.{daily,hourly}` names it
- in-repo, only `docs/infrastructure/LOGS_ORGANIZATION.md` and `scripts/SCRIPTS_ORGANIZATION.md`
  refer to it, both as inventory listings rather than callers

`/var/log/aitbc/qa-cycle.log` does not exist, in a directory holding a dozen other logs. `log()`
creates it on its first call and `main()` calls `log()` before it does anything else, so as far
as this host is concerned the script has not been invoked at all.

#### The fix

Argv lists, not `shell=True`. V23-85 cleared the B602 findings and none of
these commands needs a shell once it is a list — no pipes, no globs, no redirection, no
variable expansion:

| was | is |
| --- | --- |
| `"git fetch origin main"` | `["git", "fetch", "origin", "main"]` |
| `"git checkout main"` | `["git", "checkout", "main"]` |
| `"git reset --hard origin/main"` | `["git", "reset", "--hard", "origin/main"]` |
| `f"python3 -m pytest ... --rootdir \"$PWD\" ..."` | a literal list, `--rootdir str(package_root)` |
| `"flake8 packages/py/ --count ..."` | a literal list |

`--rootdir "$PWD"` is the one argument that needed thought rather than splitting. It is only a
shell that expands `$PWD`; the value it wanted is the `package_root` the loop is already
iterating over, so it is passed directly.

Three smaller changes, each because the bug could not have survived this long without them:

- `run_cmd` now raises `TypeError` on a string argument. The next caller written in the old
  style fails loudly instead of looking like a missing binary.
- `except Exception` narrowed to `except OSError`. That clause is what disguised the failure.
- the module-level `time.sleep(random.randint(0, 900))` moved into `main()`. At import it made
  the module impossible to import, to test, or to run with `--help` without waiting up to
  fifteen minutes first — which is a large part of why nobody ever ran this file by hand.

#### The second bug, which only became visible once the first was fixed

With `run_cmd` working, `run_tests()` reported `aitbc-sdk` failing:

```
ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'aitbc_crypto'
```

`build_pytest_env` put only the package under test and the repo root on `PYTHONPATH`. The
packages cross-import — `aitbc-sdk`'s `test_receipts.py` imports `aitbc_crypto`, which lives in
a sibling's `src/` — so the first suite failed at collection. This was never a real failure of
those packages; it was invisible for as long as pytest could not be started at all, and would
have been the script's first output the moment it could. Every `packages/py/*/src` now goes on
the path, `dict.fromkeys` de-duplicating while keeping first-wins order so the package under
test still shadows any installed copy.

#### Verification

`main()` and `fetch_latest_main()` were **not** run: they `git checkout main` and
`git reset --hard origin/main`, and this is a shared working tree that the deployed
coordinator-api, blockchain-node and edge services run from directly. Everything below was
exercised by importing the module through `importlib` with `log` monkeypatched to `print`, so
nothing was written to `/var/log/aitbc/` either.

```
argv list      -> (0, 'fix/v23-87-qa-cycle-argv\n')
nonzero exit   -> 128
missing binary -> rc -2, via the OSError path
string arg     -> TypeError: run_cmd takes an argv list, not a string: 'git fetch origin main'

the original bug, reproduced:
  string form  -> FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'git fetch origin main'
  list form    -> resolves to /usr/bin/git
```

and the two functions that had never executed:

```
Running test suites...
Testing aitbc-sdk...
✅ aitbc-sdk tests passed.
Testing aitbc-crypto...
✅ aitbc-crypto tests passed.
Running linters (flake8 if available)...
flake8 not installed; skipping lint.
```

`run_lint()` reaching its `shutil.which` guard and reporting honestly is the correct outcome
here; flake8 is not installed on this host and the repo lints with ruff.

Root suite: 6,284 passed, 272 skipped, exit 0 — unchanged from the V23-86 baseline.

`pre-commit run --all-files` fails only `require-set-euo-pipefail-in-touched-scripts`, on 329
pre-existing untouched `.sh` files, which is what that hook does under `--all-files` by design;
this change touches no shell script. Every other hook passes, `ruff`, `ruff-format` and
`mypy-clean-apps` among them.

The bandit gate from V23-85 still reports 0 results at `--severity-level medium` — 0 HIGH,
0 MEDIUM, 382 LOW, exit 0. The two `# nosec` comments added here name their rules and their
reasons (`B603` for the shell-less `subprocess.run`, `B311` for the scheduling jitter).

#### Not fixed here

`review_my_open_prs()` and `synthesize_status()` are unverified. They need `$GITEA_TOKEN`, and
`review_my_open_prs()` `POST`s to `requested_reviewers` on the live repository — running it to
see whether it works would request reviews on real open PRs. Their argument handling is not
affected by this change: they go through `query_api`, not `run_cmd`. They should be watched on
the first real invocation.

Noticed while running the gate: bandit reads everything after the rule ID in a `# nosec` comment
as further rule IDs, so `# nosec B104 - intentional service bind-all; …` makes it try to look up
`intentional`, `service`, `bind-all` and the rest. It warns once per word and ignores them, which
is harmless but adds up — 1,558 `Test in comment` warnings on a full run, enough to bury a real
message. 152 of the repo's 185 `# nosec` lines are written this way, and the two added here
follow the same house style rather than diverging from it in one file; moving
the prose onto the line above and leaving a bare `# nosec BXXX` inline silences it, and that is a
repo-wide edit rather than part of this one.


### V23-68b — the fix for a published credential, and the two ways it could not have worked · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

V23-68 removed the promotion that made `COORDINATOR_API_KEY` — a key published in a
world-readable bootstrap file — a miner credential whenever no miner keys were configured, and
told operators to set `MINER_API_KEYS` instead. Both halves of that were unreachable, and main
was left red.

#### 1. `MINER_API_KEYS` could not be read where coordinator-api is importable

```python
try:
    from coordinator_api.config import settings as config_settings

    allowed_keys = config_settings.miner_api_keys
except (ImportError, AttributeError):
    # outside coordinator-api, fall back to the raw env string for tests
    raw = os.getenv("MINER_API_KEYS", "")
```

A failed *import* was treated as the only reason to consult the environment. But `settings` is
constructed once, at import, so it holds whatever the environment said at that moment and nothing
that arrives afterwards — and wherever coordinator-api is importable, which is every service that
installs it and the whole root test suite, the environment branch is dead code. A correctly
configured miner got `401: No miner API keys configured`.

`_configured_miner_keys()` reads both sources at the moment they are needed and merges them, so
where the value came from stops mattering. An empty result still refuses everyone; that part is
the point of V23-68.

#### 2. The value the documentation asks for stopped the service from starting

```
MINER_API_KEYS=miner-one-key-value,miner-two-key-value
```

```
SettingsError: error parsing value for field "miner_api_keys" from source "EnvSettingsSource"
```

pydantic-settings JSON-decodes any complex-typed variable inside `EnvSettingsSource`, *before*
field validators run, and raises on failure. `Settings.parse_api_keys` has a comma-separated
branch, and it had never been reachable from the environment — only from code passing a string
directly. So an operator following step 5 of `docs/ops/follower-api-key.md` would not have seen
401s: they would have had a coordinator-api that does not start, because that is where its
settings are read.

`client_api_keys`, `miner_api_keys` and `admin_api_keys` are now `Annotated[list[str], NoDecode]`,
which is what hands the raw string to the validator that was written for it. Comma-separated and
JSON-array forms both load; unset stays empty.

#### The two tests that were pinning the removed fallback

Of the three failures on main, one was fault 1 above. The other two —
`tests/security/test_auth_middleware.py::test_auth_middleware_accepts_miner_api_key` and
`::test_auth_middleware_blocks_miner_key_on_admin_route` — configured the miner credential as
`COORDINATOR_API_KEY`, which is exactly what V23-68 removed. They were recording the old
behaviour and were not updated when it changed, so they failed for the correct reason. They now
configure `MINER_API_KEYS`, and a new test asserts the coordinator key is refused on
`/v1/miners/*`, so the removal is pinned rather than merely un-pinned.
`test_auth_middleware_rejects_invalid_api_key` had also been passing for the wrong reason — no
keys were configured at all, so it never reached a comparison.

#### What operators have to do

`/etc/aitbc/aitbc-coordinator-api.env` on this host sets `COORDINATOR_API_KEY` and no
`MINER_API_KEYS`. The deployed coordinator-api runs from this working tree, so **at its next
restart every `X-Api-Key` request to the miner, settlement and marketplace routers answers 401**
until a miner key is configured. That is V23-68 working as intended, not a regression introduced
here, and it is the reason step 5 of the ops doc now says "before the next restart" and gives the
format.

#### Verification

Root suite: **6,593 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, 271 skipped, exit 0** — green again, from 3
failures on `dd250092e`. The five added tests are the three `Settings` parsing forms, the
coordinator-key refusal on `/v1/miners/*`, and `_configured_miner_keys()` reading the environment
while coordinator-api is importable, which is the condition under which it could not.

`ruff`, `ruff format` and `mypy` clean over `aitbc/auth/dependencies.py` and
`apps/coordinator-api/src/coordinator_api/config.py`.

#### Not fixed here

`AuthMiddleware` catches any `HTTPException` from the dependency and rewrites it to
`{"detail": "Invalid API key"}`, so `No miner API keys configured` never reaches the client. An
operator debugging a 401 after a restart sees a message about the key they sent rather than the
list that is missing.


